> assume a byte is unsigned
  ok: a byte is unsigned
> what is the range of x for a byte x?
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: range it
range of it -> u8:
  0..255   (256 distinct values)
  fits in u8
  over all 256 points of the domain
> assume bytes are signed
  ok: bytes are signed
> what is the range of x plus 1 for a byte x?
  ucas: it(x: i8) = x + 1
  ucas: range it
range of it -> i8:
  -128..127   (256 distinct values)
  fits in i8
  over all 256 points of the domain
> does x times x overflow for a 16-bit unsigned x?
  ucas: it(x: u16) = x*x
  ucas: wrap it
wrap analysis of it:
  x*x   (u16)
      nuw fails: unsigned wrap at 65280 points, e.g. x=256
      nsw fails: signed wrap at 65173 points, e.g. x=182
  end to end: differs from the mathematical result at 65280 points, e.g. x=256
      exact for: 0..255
  over all 65536 points of the domain
> what is the range of x divided by 2 for a 32-bit signed x?
  ucas: it(x: i32) = x/2
  ucas: range it
range of it -> i32:
  -1073741824..1073741823  (sampled min/max; not a sound bound)
  fits in i31
  sampled 65536 points (domain has 4294967296 points); not exhaustive
  sound bound (intervals): -2147483648..2147483647
> what is the range of n for an integer n between 1 and 12?
  note: "integer" without a width: read as an unbounded integer, int, not a C int, i32 (say "32-bit integer", or "assume integers are 32-bit")
  ucas: it(n: int in 1..12) = n
  ucas: range it
range of it -> int:
  1..12
  fits in u4
  over all 12 points of the domain
> what is the range of 2 to the n for n between 0 and 10?
  ucas: it(n in 0..10) = 2^n
  ucas: range it
range of it -> int:
  1..1024
  fits in u11
  over all 11 points of the domain
> forget everything
  ok: forgot every assumption
> what is the range of x for a byte x?
  note: "byte" without signed or unsigned: read as unsigned, u8, not signed, i8 (say "unsigned byte", or "assume bytes are signed")
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: range it
range of it -> u8:
  0..255   (256 distinct values)
  fits in u8
  over all 256 points of the domain
> assume int means i32
  ok: int means i32
> does x plus 1 overflow for an int x?
  ucas: it(x: i32) = x + 1
  ucas: wrap it
wrap analysis of it:
  x + 1   (i32)
      nuw fails: unsigned wrap at 1 points, e.g. x=-1
      nsw fails: signed wrap at 1 points, e.g. x=2147483647  <- UB in C; a compiler assumes it cannot happen
  end to end: differs from the mathematical result at 1 points, e.g. x=2147483647
  sampled 65536 points (domain has 4294967296 points); not exhaustive
> assume int means unbounded
  ok: int means unbounded
> does x plus 1 overflow for an int x?
  ucas: it(x: int) = x + 1
  ucas: wrap it
wrap analysis of it:
  no wrapping or rounding operations
  end to end: equals the mathematical (unbounded) result everywhere
  sampled 65536 points (domain has 2^64 or more points); not exhaustive
